  "syPiholeChanges": "What changes at home, and how to go back",
  "syPiholeDevice": "If your router does not let you change the DNS (some providers' routers do not), set it on each device instead: in the Wi-Fi network's details on phones, in the network adapter's settings on computers.",
  "syPiholeDomain": "Your domain (optional)",
  "syPiholeDomainHelp": "The address you use from outside, like rasgeth.yourdomain.com: Pi-hole answers for it with the address above. Leave it empty to use Pi-hole only to block ads.",
  "syPiholeDomainWrong": "It must be a name like rasgeth.yourdomain.com.",
  "syPiholeIp": "This computer's address at home",
  "syPiholeIpHelp": "Four numbers like 192.168.1.50. Find it on the router's page among the connected devices, or on this computer: \"ip a\" on Linux, \"ipconfig\" on Windows, System Settings, Network on Mac. Better if the router always gives this computer the same address (address reservation).",
  "syPiholeIpWrong": "It must be four numbers from 0 to 255 separated by dots, like 192.168.1.50.",
  "syPiholeNeed": "you reach Rasgeth from outside with your own domain, and at home the same link does not open. Many routers cannot send you back to your own house through the internet (the feature is called NAT loopback or hairpin, and theirs lacks it).",
  "syPiholeNeedTitle": "You need it if",
  "syPiholeNoNeed": "you use Tailscale or Cloudflare Tunnel: those links work the same at home and outside. You can still turn it on just to block ads, if you like.",
  "syPiholeNoNeedTitle": "You do not need it if",
  "syPiholeNowRouter": "Last step, yours: on your router (or on each device) set the DNS server to {ip}. Until then nothing at home uses Pi-hole.",
  "@syPiholeNowRouter": {
    "placeholders": {
      "ip": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "syPiholeOff": "To go back: put the router's DNS back as it was (often \"automatic\") and turn Pi-hole off here. Nothing else on your computers changes.",
  "syPiholeOn": "Pi-hole is on, at {ip}.",
  "@syPiholeOn": {
    "placeholders": {
      "ip": {
        "type": "String"
      }
    }
  },
  "syPiholeOpenAdmin": "Open Pi-hole",
  "syPiholePort": "Technical details: port 53",
  "syPiholePortText": "Pi-hole answers on port 53 (the DNS port) of this computer's home address only, not on the internet, and its own page on port 8053. If something on this computer already answers on port 53 (another DNS, or the system's own on some Linux systems), Pi-hole cannot start: Rasgeth says so and nothing changes.",
  "syPiholeRouter": "For every device at once: open your router's page (its address is usually written under the router, like 192.168.1.1), find the DHCP or LAN settings and set the DNS server to this computer's home address. Devices pick it up the next time they connect (or restart them).",
  "syPiholeSecond": "A second DNS: if this computer is off, devices that only know Pi-hole cannot open any site. As a second DNS you can put your router's address or a public one (1.1.1.1): everything keeps working when this computer is off, but devices may sometimes ask the second one and then see ads, or not find your domain at home.",
  "syPiholeShowPassword": "Show its password",
  "syPiholeStart": "Turn on Pi-hole",
  "syPiholeStop": "Turn off Pi-hole",
  "syPiholeTest": "How to check: connect your phone to the home Wi-Fi and open your Rasgeth link. If it opens, you do not need Pi-hole for this (you may still want it to block ads). If it does not, but works on mobile data, Pi-hole fixes it.",
  "syPiholeTitle": "The home's own DNS, with Pi-hole (optional)",
  "syPiholeWhat": "Every device at home asks a DNS server which number is behind a name (for example rasgeth.yourdomain.com). Usually your router or your internet provider answers. Pi-hole is a small DNS that Rasgeth can run on this computer: it answers for your domain with this computer's home address, so the link works at home too, and it also blocks advertising and tracking for every device that uses it.",
  "syPwSignOutOthersQ": "Sign out your other devices too?",
  "syPwSignOutOthersText": "The other browsers and the apps signed in with the old password stay in if you keep them. Sign them out if you changed it because someone else might know it.",
  "syPwSignOutOthersYes": "Sign them out",
  "syRemoteCaddy": "For experts: your domain pointing at home, ports 80 and 443 opened on the router. Anyone on the internet can reach the sign-in page.",
  "syRemoteCloudflare": "An address on your own domain (about 10 euros a year), nothing opened on the router. A few more steps on Cloudflare's site.",
  "syRemoteCode": "Second check (recommended)",
  "syRemoteCodeConfirm": "Confirm",
